                           THE ENABLE2K ABLE SUPPLEMENT
�����������������������������������������������������������������������������


The ABLE supplement to ENABLE2K is a collection of files relating to the
ABLE (Alternate British LExicon) word list to be found in the ENABLE2K
Supplement.  The ABLE word list purports to contain words from the fourth
edition of the British Official Scrabble Words (OSW) and the 1997
Chambers Dictionary.  As distributed in the ENABLE2K supplement, it was
shortened by removing words duplicated in the main ENABLE2K WORD.LST.
This supplement contains the list in its entirety.

Since the release of the ABLE list in 2000, I have been engaged in
verifying its contents, and making corrections as necessary.  The
results, present in this archive as FULLABLE.LST, do not entirely
reflect OSW for two reasons.  First, some OSW words are not eligible for
Scrabble play according to American rules, and have been removed.  Also,
a small number of cases have been found where OSW and Chambers are in
conflict, or where OSW omits words I believe to be implied by, or at
least compatible with, Chambers.  The ineligible words are to be found
in the file UNABLE.LST.  The corrections, with annotations, are to be
found in the file ABLESIG.LST.  Both files are discussed in greater
detail below.

Words were moved from FULLABLE.LST to UNABLE.LST for one of three
reasons preventing their use in American Scrabble play.  Most of the
words in UNABLE.LST are shown by Chambers as unassimilated foreign
words, such as "bitte", "gentilhomme", "kulak" and "tubae".  Another
Scrabble requirement is that a word must have a part of speech.  This
causes words like "gotta", "winna" and "wysiwyg" to be dropped.
Interestingly enough, most words in this category are Latin, such as
"dixi" and "fecit", and therefore excluded by the first criterion
as well.  Finally, the Scrabble rules prohibit "capitalized" words.
OSW includes the words "pH" and "pHs", on the grounds that they are not
capitalized in the strictest sense of the word.  I hold that the intent
of this provision is to exclude words containing any capital letters,
as otherwise, words like "cDNA". "dGTP" and "rRNA" would be listed in
OSPD.  For this reason, I have moved "pH" and its plural to UNABLE.LST.

The ABLESIG.LST file of "signature corrections" contains 356 words.
Each word begins with a character indicating the action required for
the word.  A "+" indicates a word which was added, and a "-" one which
was removed.  A "&" indicates an additional word which, though present
or implied by Chambers, is not valid for Scrabble play as discussed
above.  A "~" indicates a removed word which, if it were to be
accepted, would be invalid for Scrabble play.  The FULLABLE.LST and
UNABLE.LST files have had the changes indicated by ABLESIG.LST already
applied.  Thus, if you do not accept these changes, the edits should be
undone.  For instance, the signature word "clast" (shown with a "+")
would be removed from FULLABLE.LST, and the word "chilioi" (shown with
a "~") would be added to UNABLE.LST.  (See the ENABLE2K Supplement for
information on the MAKELIST program, which is provided to facilitate
such recombination of the ENABLE2K lists.)

While there are too many words in ABLESIG.LST to justify them all, I will
discuss briefly some classes of corrections, and some interesting
specific cases.  I will also discuss a few ambiguous entries in Chambers
for which I concluded that no correction was warranted.  As you will
observe from these "war stories", the Chambers dictionary is a most
unusual dictionary from an American perspective, and the high proportion
of archaic, literary, Scots, and just plain peculiar words therein
presents considerable challenges of interpretation.  (During my original
editing of the ENABLE word list, I referred to MW10 as "the big book of
drugs, chemicals and diseases," out of frustration with its seeming
emphasis on esoteric scientific terminology.  Chambers, in contrast,
could easily be called "the humongous book of Spenser, Scots and the
Raj," even though it likely surpasses MW10 in drugs, chemicals and
diseases as well.)

The most common class of corrections in ABLESIG.TXT contains what I call
"etc." words.  These derive from entries such as that for "ecumenic",
which defines six words and concludes "Also oecumenic, etc.".  My
assumption is that the "etc." (and others) should be taken as meaningful,
and that the existence of "oecumenical", "oecumenicalism", and so on, is
implied.  Prefixes such as "archeo-", "lacri-" and "leuko-" generate
large numbers of such words.  The inclusion by OSW of "spagerist" and
"spagirist" is an unusual case where the compilers of OSW have given
such a reading of "etc.", which may lend some legitimacy to it.

Another common class of corrections contains additional inflections for
words for which Chambers does not list the inflections.  The introduction
to OSW admits that, where necessary, its compilers have gone beyond
Chambers for inflections; thus the inclusion of words like "hymenia",
which is not actually present in Chambers.  I have tried, in continuing
this process, to rely as much as possible on British sources, especially
the New Shorter Oxford English Dictionary (NSOED), but I have used
American sources where I couldn't find British ones.  I have avoided
adding irregular disease plurals (e.g., "arthritides"), having been
assured by an expert that these plurals are not used in British English.
Examples of plurals not in OSW that I have added are "encephala", "litui",
"matzohs" and "victrices".  "encephala", a plural of "encephalon", is
worth mentioning because of the significant number of related compounds
such as "diencephalon", "rhinencephalon", etc.  I have listed the -a
plurals of all these words in ABLESIG.TXT, so that anyone who believes
that "encephala" was deliberately excluded from OSW can more easily
remove all these related words.

I found it amusing that OSW shows, for some words considered foreign,
only a regular English plural.  I therefore made an effort to determine
any appropriate non-English plurals for these words, and added them to
ABLESIG.TXT when successful, resulting in such additions as "autobahnen",
"gighe" and "kulaki".

Another source of signature corrections was the frequent practice in
Chambers of describing a word as a variant spelling without specifying
the parts of speech for which the variant was used.  While sometimes this
could be resolved by considering, for instance, that none of Spenser's
works related to computers, there were still many ambiguities, generally
involving historical spellings.  I used the NSOED and, for a few
particularly difficult cases, the full OED, to resolve such questions,
resulting in the addition of words like "antickes", "gingled", "gingling",
"poudred" and "poudring", and the removal of "wheeshed", "wheeshes" and
"wheeshing".

Some interesting corrections made for other reasons include:

agonothete - Chambers lists "agonothetes" as "a director of public games".
   But NSOED shows the word as "agonothete".  I have chosen to regard
   this as an error in Chambers, and added "agonothete".
airbus - Clearly OSW is in error here, since it shows "airbusses", but
   not "airbus" or "airbuses".  What is unclear is whether "airbusses"
   should be removed, or "airbus" added.  "air-bus" is listed under
   "air", implying that "airbus" is erroneous, but it is also used
   without hyphen under "aerobus", which persuaded me to add rather
   than remove.  Similar reasons led to the addition of "cruiserweight".
centimorgan - The most recent Chambers shows this word as "centi Morgan",
   but earlier editions showed "centimorgan".  The two-word form is
   very odd, and I could find no corroboration for it, so I added
   back "centimorgan" as a signature.
chondroma - This word is shown (under the heading "chondrus") only in the
   plural, but there is no reason not to infer the signular.  "clast" and
   "frunctan" were added for similar reasons.  I believe I found a case
   where OSW includes such an implied singular, but I have misplaced it.
francomania, etc. - The entry for "Franco-" includes the subentry
   "n Francomania, Francophil, Francophile, Francophobe and Francophobia
   (also without cap)."  It isn't clear whether the "also without cap"
   applies to the entire list or not, but I have chosen to assume that
   it does.  (It seems unlikely to me that "francophobia" would be
   valid, but "francophobe" not.)
piercings - This word was removed because "piercing" is not shown as a
   noun, but only as a component of the phrase "body piercing".  The
   word "rouming" was removed for similar reasons.
pizzazz - This word, and its many variants, is presumably omitted from
   OSW because, having four z's, it is impossible to play in Scrabble.
   Since ENABLE2K intentionally does not limit itself exclusively to
   Scrabble, these words have been added.
wilco - Chambers' entry for this word shows it as an interjection, but
   calls it an abbreviation in the text.  Since "abbrev" is not shown
   as the part of speech, I have chosen to regard it as a legitimate
   word.  (One might question why I chose to add "wilco", but not "sinh",
   whose entry is similar.  Good question.)

Some examples of corrections considered but ultimately rejected are as
follows:

alphatest - Chambers shows the verb "alphatest", which is omitted from
   OSW.  This is dubious, and I have chosen to leave it out, based on
   the spelling of the corresponding form "beta-test".
peincted - Chambers shows "peinct" as an obsolete spelling of "painting".
   This seems unlikely.  The OED confirms "peinct" as an old spelling
   of "paint", implying the correctness of OSW's inclusion of "peincted"
   and "peincting".
sinoekete - The 1997 Chambers lists the word "sinoekete" as a variant of
   "synoecete".  Previous editions listed "synoekete", which is the form
   in OSW.  Since NSOED lists the -y- spelling, but not the -i- spelling,
   I believe that the 1997 entry is a typo, and have retained "synoekete".
   Similar considerations have led me to reject the Chambers "xeraphin"
   in favor of the OSW "xeraphim".
tottring - The 1997 Chambers lists "tottring" as a Shakespearean word
   meaning "hanging in rags".  Previous editions listed the word as
   "tott'ring", which of course is ineligible for OSW.  The literary
   archives confirm that the correct form is "tott'ring", and so
   "tottring" was not added.
whatsoeer - The 1997 Chambers shows a number of words ending in "eer"
   such as "whatsoeer", "whensoeer" and "wheneer" that previous
   editions showed with an "e'er" ending.  I could find no confirmation
   of these forms, and they are not in OSW.  But the fact that a number
   of such words changed makes it seem less likely this is just a typo.
   My best guess is that the problem is caused by the extreme similarity
   of the apostrophe and the syllable accent in the Chambers typeface.
   At any rate, without some confirmation of these forms, I was unable
   to persuade myself to add them.


Finally, I should note two things.  One is that the Chambers dictionary
is a mammoth work.  While I have done my best to accurately reflect every
obscure entry, is it not possible that I have avoided mistakes, even
ignoring questions of interpretation without a clear resolution.  The
ABLE list is still far from perfect; the most I can claim is that it is
slightly improved from the previous version.

In compiling this version of the ABLE list, I have received the advice of
a number of anonymous experts (you know who you are).  I would like to
offer my thanks here; without your help, many details of this project
would have been less than satisfactorily resolved.  Of course, I myself
take full blame for any remaining errors.



